    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media, from conservative to left‑leaning outlets, uniformly stress that the accelerating digitalisation and automation of industry are creating a measurable skills shortage and that systematic reskilling programmes – especially in AI, robotics and advanced manufacturing – are now a prerequisite for maintaining Germany’s competitiveness. At the same time, there is a cross‑political consensus that wage policy, whether through the statutory minimum wage or sectoral collective‑bargaining, must be adjusted to reflect the rising cost of living and the growing share of low‑paid and platform‑based jobs, prompting calls for reforms of the minijob system and stronger enforcement of equal‑pay standards. Finally, despite divergent views on the role of unions, all major strands of coverage agree that works councils and collective representation are essential safeguards for workers’ rights as the labour market becomes more fragmented and precarious.",
      "controversial_points": "The clash between market liberalism and collective bargaining defines the wage debate. Automation fuels tension between productivity gains and job security demands of unions and works councils. Basic income proposals pit fiscal conservatives against social democrats over redistribution and welfare sustainability. Platform work creates conflict between flexible labor models favored by startups and protectionist regulations demanded by labor movements."
